{
  "rules": [
    "Correct for severe mean reversion: The model consistently fails to anticipate sharp price drops following short-term spikes (e.g., the drop from ~76 to ~62 in Example 1 and ~76 to ~67 in Examples 2-5). Reduce forecasts by 10-15% immediately after any 3-day upward trend exceeding 5%.",
    "Adjust for sentiment lag: Positive sentiment spikes (value 1.00) are followed by immediate price surges that the model misses; increase the first 3-5 days of the forecast by 3-5% when sentiment history contains a 1.00 value.",
    "Eliminate flat-line bias: The model exhibits excessive inertia, predicting near-constant prices regardless of volatility. Replace the model's flat trajectory with a dynamic path that mirrors the historical volatility range (standard deviation) of the last 10 days.",
    "Horizon-specific decay: The model overestimates prices in the 15-30 day horizon during downtrends. Apply a linear decay factor of -0.5% per day to the forecast starting from day 10 if the 10-day moving average is trending downward.",
    "Overshoot correction: After a price spike, the model fails to account for the subsequent 'hangover' effect where prices fall below the pre-spike average. Ensure the 20-day forecast target is at least 5% below the peak price reached in the preceding 10 days."
  ]
}
